WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.969
-My first guest is
an Emmy winner, a Tony winner,

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:05.438
and a "New York Times"
best-selling author.

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:07.307
He is the star
and executive producer

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:09.308
of the Showtime series
"Your Honor,"

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:11.344
which airs Sunday nights
at 10:00 p.m.

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:14.346
Here he is, the one and only
Bryan Cranston!

00:00:14.346 --> 00:00:17.349
Ah, look at you!
-Hey, Jimmy.

00:00:17.349 --> 00:00:20.186
-You are the best.
You're looking good, buddy.

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:21.387
We love it when you stop by,

00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:24.557
and we appreciate it
always, always, always.

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:28.160
Thank you for going above and
beyond and doing "Fancy Soaps."

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.562
That was fun.

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:32.499
-It just so happens that
I am a fancy soap collector,

00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:34.167
and you didn't even know that.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:35.601
I did not know that.
-Amazing.

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:38.971
-I didn't realize this, but soap
actually plays a big part

00:00:38.971 --> 00:00:40.940
in your acting career.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.110
One of your first gigs
was a soap commercial,

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:45.611
and we have a photo.

00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:49.114
Here's a shot of Bryan Cranston
in his Shield Soap commercial.

00:00:49.114 --> 00:00:50.883
That's a stud right there.
Come on.

00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:52.218
Do you remember
getting this gig?

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:53.819
-Yeah, look at that young man.
Yeah, I do remember.

00:00:53.819 --> 00:00:55.221
[ Clears throat ]
In order to get

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:56.322
a soap commercial,

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:58.323
you got to go in really stinky.
-Ah.

00:00:58.323 --> 00:01:00.125
-That's good to know.

00:01:00.125 --> 00:01:05.331
-I mean, yeah, it is after big
garlic meal the night before...

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:07.633
-Yeah, I got you.
-...and a few drinks stinky

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:10.536
and then running in sweats
and then coming in,

00:01:10.536 --> 00:01:13.239
because then they have to show
that the product really works.

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.241
-Something else I did not
really know about you --

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.276
but before your acting career
took off,

00:01:17.276 --> 00:01:20.779
you were briefly
a stand-up comedian.

00:01:20.779 --> 00:01:22.748
What got you into stand-up?
Do you remember?

00:01:22.748 --> 00:01:27.753
-Yeah. You know, Jimmy,
it comes more naturally to you,

00:01:27.753 --> 00:01:29.188
but it didn't come naturally
to me.

00:01:29.188 --> 00:01:30.523
I was afraid of it.

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:32.625
I think the only reason that
I thought

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:35.928
I should do some stand-up
is because it frightened me,

00:01:35.928 --> 00:01:40.266
and just the idea
of you're alone onstage,

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:44.303
a microphone and a light
shining on you, and that's it.

00:01:44.303 --> 00:01:45.804
Go.

00:01:45.804 --> 00:01:48.807
And I thought, "Wow, to be able
to do that would be amazing."

00:01:48.807 --> 00:01:51.945
So I took a class and started
writing some material

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:53.612
and went around
to The Comedy Store

00:01:53.612 --> 00:01:55.915
and the Improv
and The Laff Stop

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:58.117
and the Playboy Club
out here in Los Angeles

00:01:58.117 --> 00:01:59.752
and did the routine.

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:02.021
I never got paid for it,

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:04.290
nor should I
ever have been paid for it.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:05.524
-Nope.

00:02:05.524 --> 00:02:09.395
-I just did the open-mic nights,
and I rose from --

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:13.599
I rose all the way up to
the lofty position of mediocre.

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:15.401
-Oh. [ Laughs ]
Congratulations.

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:16.635
Oh, my God.
-I got up.

00:02:16.635 --> 00:02:19.572
-Wow. You got all the way up
to mediocre, yeah.

00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:21.740
-Yeah.
-Do you remember any of your --

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:25.578
any of your set at all,
any jokes?

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:28.414
-Well, the one
that really worked the best

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:30.283
was when I was talking about

00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:34.854
how the plight of car sales
in the country have gone down

00:02:34.854 --> 00:02:38.824
and, you know,
particularly men --

00:02:38.824 --> 00:02:41.160
much more women
are buying cars than men.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.463
I said there's an easy way
for men to buy cars --

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:45.498
just name them
after women's body parts.

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:47.566
It's like...
-Oh, yeah.

00:02:47.566 --> 00:02:49.836
-"Guys, how about
the perky little Ford Nipple?"

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:50.903
-[ Laughs and applauds ]
Yeah.

00:02:50.903 --> 00:02:52.538
-"That's an adorable
little guy."

00:02:52.538 --> 00:02:54.173
-Dude, that's actually
a good bit.

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:56.275
That would work right now,
by the way.

00:02:56.275 --> 00:02:58.243
That would totally work
right now.

00:02:58.243 --> 00:03:00.213
I see already it already.

00:03:00.213 --> 00:03:02.481
Well, earlier in your intro,
I was -- I was --

00:03:02.481 --> 00:03:04.050
I was listing your awards.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.418
You won a Tony.
You won an Emmy.

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:07.686
You're a "New York Times"
best-selling author.

00:03:07.686 --> 00:03:09.121
But you've won another award --

00:03:09.121 --> 00:03:12.258
you have
an award-winning mezcal.

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:15.194
-Yeah.
-This is major.

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:19.532
-We just won Double Gold, yeah,
from "Cigar &amp; Spirits Magazine."

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:23.402
Dos Hombres, and I --
-Dos Hombres Mezcal.

00:03:23.402 --> 00:03:25.705
-Dos Hombres.
-And you and Aaron Paul

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:27.907
got together,
and you guys started --

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:30.643
How did that happen?
I love this.

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:35.147
-You know, about three years
after the end of "Breaking Bad,"

00:03:35.147 --> 00:03:37.383
we were in New York together,
having dinner,

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:40.152
and we hadn't seen each other
a lot since then.

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:41.887
It was, "God, I miss you.

00:03:41.887 --> 00:03:43.890
You know, we used to see
each other all the time,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.493
and it was so intimate, and
we were really good friends,"

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:49.896
but life goes on
and you move on to other things.

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:51.030
And he said,
"You know, what we ought to do

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.465
is start a mezcal company."

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:54.500
And I went, "You're kidding.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:55.735
The thing with the worm
at the bottom?"

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:58.938
He goes, "No, that's
a BS marketing thing, no.

00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:00.139
I mean, really good mezcal."
-No, but you're right.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:01.707
You're right -- It was that.
That was it.

00:04:01.707 --> 00:04:04.043
That's what I knew it as.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:07.279
-Yeah, that was
horrible and cheap.

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:09.015
This is unbelievable.

00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:10.716
He took me to a mezcal bar.

00:04:10.716 --> 00:04:12.418
We tasted a bunch
of different kinds,

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:14.487
and I said, "This is delicious."

00:04:14.487 --> 00:04:16.289
We went down to Oaxaca
for a week

00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:19.525
and tested and tasted
about 80 different mezcals

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:21.560
until we found Dos Hombres.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:25.764
And it is -- it's just
a beautiful spirit...

00:04:25.764 --> 00:04:28.433
-Just delicious and smoky.
-...old-world, gluten-free --

00:04:28.433 --> 00:04:29.669
it's fantastic.

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:31.904
-I have some,
and it's actually delicious.

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:33.373
I love it.
-It's delicious.

00:04:33.373 --> 00:04:34.440
-But that's crazy
that you just do that

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.308
just out of love
of missing each other

00:04:36.308 --> 00:04:38.076
and you go,
"Yeah, let's just do this

00:04:38.076 --> 00:04:40.079
so we're forced to talk
to each other and hang out."

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:41.547
-[ Laughs ] So --

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:43.883
Yeah, but there's reason
for us to talk to each other

00:04:43.883 --> 00:04:45.617
every day, yeah.

00:04:45.617 --> 00:04:48.721
-Hey, I want to talk about your
Showtime series "Your Honor."

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:51.791
For those who haven't seen it,
can you set this up?

00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:53.593
-Yeah.

00:04:53.593 --> 00:04:57.797
"Your Honor" is about a superior
court judge and his son.

00:04:57.797 --> 00:05:00.833
His son is 17, a sweet kid,

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:03.069
and he unfortunately
gets involved

00:05:03.069 --> 00:05:06.305
in a traffic accident
where a boy dies,

00:05:06.305 --> 00:05:10.476
and he panics, and he leaves
the scene of the accident,

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:14.146
thereby becoming a crime scene.

00:05:14.146 --> 00:05:17.115
When he tells me
and confesses what happened,

00:05:17.115 --> 00:05:18.851
I tell him, "You've got
to do the right thing.

00:05:18.851 --> 00:05:20.619
You have to be accountable
for your actions."

00:05:20.619 --> 00:05:22.388
And I convince him --

00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:24.123
we're going to turn him in
to the police

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:25.924
and go through the system.

00:05:25.924 --> 00:05:27.526
When we get to
the police station,

00:05:27.526 --> 00:05:32.064
I notice the parents of the boy
who died that morning,

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:37.236
and the man, the father,
is a vicious mob boss,

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:40.673
and I know that
that man will kill my son,

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:43.642
regardless of whose fault
it was or anything.

00:05:43.642 --> 00:05:45.344
And so,
with that new information,

00:05:45.344 --> 00:05:50.515
I realize I can't just
turn my son in to be killed --

00:05:50.515 --> 00:05:52.017
I have to protect him.

00:05:52.017 --> 00:05:54.787
So we do an about-face,
and we leave.

00:05:54.787 --> 00:05:56.956
-Yeah.
-And from that point on,

00:05:56.956 --> 00:06:01.093
we reverse-engineer
everything that happened,

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:05.497
creating alibis,
destroying evidence,

00:06:05.497 --> 00:06:08.734
manipulating the jury system,
and the whole thing,

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:11.704
and, of course,
it's a downward spiral.

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:15.207
It's very tense, very emotional.
-Yeah.

00:06:15.207 --> 00:06:17.176
-I just didn't thank that
the society had

00:06:17.176 --> 00:06:19.111
enough anxiety in their lives,

00:06:19.111 --> 00:06:20.746
so, like, we're contributing.
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:20.746 --> 00:06:22.781
You think people need more? No.
But by the way...

00:06:22.781 --> 00:06:25.618
-We need more.
-...it's a hit, and congrats.

00:06:25.618 --> 00:06:30.689
And I know
you direct the finale.

00:06:30.689 --> 00:06:32.524
-Yeah.
-Coming up.

00:06:32.524 --> 00:06:35.661
-Yeah, I direct the 10th episode
of it, and it's --

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:40.633
you know, it is very interesting
directing yourself.

00:06:40.633 --> 00:06:43.302
The director me will
always approach the actor me

00:06:43.302 --> 00:06:45.004
and say something like,

00:06:45.004 --> 00:06:47.373
"Wow, I mean,
you're a lot better-looking

00:06:47.373 --> 00:06:49.208
than I thought you were."
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:50.643
-And...
-Slow clap.

00:06:50.643 --> 00:06:53.779
-And then, the actor me
gets flustered

00:06:53.779 --> 00:06:56.615
and feels the flirtation
and sleeps with the director.

00:06:56.615 --> 00:06:58.617
-Ohh.
-It's kind of nasty, really.

00:06:58.617 --> 00:07:00.619
-How long have you been
sleeping with the director?

00:07:00.619 --> 00:07:02.922
-Oh, many years.
-Wow.

00:07:02.922 --> 00:07:04.690
-And it hasn't gotten me
very far.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:08.827
I'm always disappointed, too.
-[ Laughs ] Oh, my gosh.

00:07:08.827 --> 00:07:10.863
-Really.
It's like, "Is that it?

00:07:10.863 --> 00:07:12.331
Really? That's all?"
-That was all.

00:07:12.331 --> 00:07:13.599
-Hm.
-Yeah.

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:14.667
-I liked the flirting better.

00:07:14.667 --> 00:07:17.569
Well, here's Bryan Cranston...
-Yeah.

00:07:17.569 --> 00:07:19.772
-...not necessarily directed
by Bryan Cranston,

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:21.306
in "Your Honor."

00:07:21.306 --> 00:07:22.774
Take a look at this.

00:07:22.774 --> 00:07:28.681
-12 of our peers will do
exactly what I want them to do.

00:07:31.217 --> 00:07:35.353
Tomorrow or the next day,
I am making a move.

00:07:35.353 --> 00:07:36.956
Now, are we done here?

00:07:39.758 --> 00:07:43.028
-Only number in here is mine.

00:07:43.028 --> 00:07:47.866
I want to know what you've done
and when you've done it,

00:07:47.866 --> 00:07:52.505
and I want it by 10:00 a.m.
the day after tomorrow.

00:07:52.505 --> 00:07:57.009
Let's call that a deadline,

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:59.478
and let's give that word
its literal meaning.

00:07:59.478 --> 00:08:05.518
♪♪

00:08:05.518 --> 00:08:06.785
-Bryan Cranston, everybody.

00:08:06.785 --> 00:08:08.020
Check out "Your Honor"

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:10.690
Sunday nights at 10:00 p.m.
on Showtime.

